window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"948fb4b64927e18616b40c7f5a8cd68af8d9765bcba86ced6e966914a28a4570":{"markdown":"## 4. A proof of both boundary limits\n\nWeak convergence at the lower edge would not suffice. We prove norm convergence on every vector and on every adjoint vector by explicit scalar kernels. For \\(0<s<1\\) define\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.ps7\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nP_s^0(r)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}{2(\\cosh(\\pi r)-\\cos(\\pi s))},\\qquad\nP_s^1(r)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}{2(\\cosh(\\pi r)+\\cos(\\pi s))}=P_{1-s}^0(r).\n\\tag{PS7}\n\\]\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l158.poisson-masses\"><\/a>\n\nThey are positive. With \\(\\theta=\\pi s,\\quad v=e^{\\pi r}\\), direct one-variable integration gives\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.ps8\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}P_s^0(r)\\,dr\n=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty\n\\frac{dv}{(v-\\cos\\theta)^2+\\sin^2\\theta}\n=1-s,\n\\qquad\\int_{\\mathbb R}P_s^1(r)\\,dr=s.\n\\tag{PS8}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\(\\delta>0\\) the tail of \\(P_s^0\\) outside \\((-\\delta,\\delta)\\) is at most\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.ps9\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}2\\int_{|r|\\geq\\delta}\n\\frac{dr}{\\cosh(\\pi r)-1},\n\\tag{PS9}\n\\]\n\nwhich tends to zero as \\(s\\) decreases to zero. The fixed comparison integral is finite, away from its singular point and with exponential decay. At the lower edge exchange \\(s\\) with \\(1-s\\) and the two labels.\n\nFor bounded continuous scalar \\(b_0,b_1\\), convolution with these two kernels is a bounded harmonic function of \\((t,s)\\). To verify harmonicity directly, the kernels are respectively \\(-1/2\\) times the imaginary part of \\(\\coth(\\pi(r+is)/2)\\) and \\(1/2\\) times the imaginary part of \\(\\tanh(\\pi(r+is)/2)\\). They have no poles in the open strip. On compact interior substrips, their derivatives through order two have integrable bounds with exponential tails. Differentiating the kernels in the convolution variable \\(t-x\\) under the scalar integral is therefore legitimate. The masses and (PS9) prove joint convergence to \\(b_0(t_0)\\) as \\((t,s)\\to(t_0,0)\\): split the first convolution into small \\(|r|\\), controlled by continuity, and its tail, controlled by (PS9); the second convolution has mass \\(s\\). The same argument works at the bottom.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l158.poisson-uniqueness\"><\/a>\n\nThe bounded harmonic function with these edge data is unique. For a bounded harmonic difference \\(q\\) with zero edge values, compare its real part and its negative with\n\n\\[\n\\epsilon\\cosh(ct)\\cos(c(s-1/2)),\\qquad 0<c<\\pi.\n\\]\n\nThis is positive harmonic on the closed strip. On the distant vertical sides of a sufficiently wide rectangle it exceeds \\(|\\operatorname{Re}q|\\); on the horizontal edges it exceeds zero. The rectangle harmonic maximum principle gives the comparison in the rectangle. Let \\(\\varepsilon\\) decrease to zero at each fixed point and repeat for \\(\\operatorname{Im}q\\). Thus every bounded scalar holomorphic function continuous on the closed strip equals the stated convolution of its edges.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l158.vector-boundaries\"><\/a>\n\nApply this scalar identity to the coefficients of \\(V\\). SF-3 constructs the required improper vector integrals directly, since the boundary orbits are bounded continuous and the kernels are continuous integrable. Equality of all scalar inner products proves\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.ps10\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(t-is)\\xi\n&=\\int P_s^0(r)A(t-r)\\xi\\,dr+\\int P_s^1(r)B(t-r)\\xi\\,dr,\\\\\nV(t-is)^*\\xi\n&=\\int P_s^0(r)A(t-r)^*\\xi\\,dr+\\int P_s^1(r)B(t-r)^*\\xi\\,dr.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{PS10}\n\\]\n\nFor the second line pair the first with two vectors and use the real-valued kernels; both adjoint orbits are norm-continuous on each vector. The approximate-identity proof uses only the integral norm bound, so it now proves both vector limits jointly. More explicitly, for \\(|t-t_0|<\\delta\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\rho_\\xi(\\delta)=\\sup_{|q-t_0|\\leq2\\delta}\n\\|(A(q)-A(t_0))\\xi\\|,\n\\]\n\none has\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.ps11\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\|(V(t-is)-A(t_0))\\xi\\|\n\\leq\\rho_\\xi(\\delta)\n+2\\|\\xi\\|\\int_{|r|\\geq\\delta}P_s^0(r)\\,dr\n+2s\\|\\xi\\|.\n\\tag{PS11}\n\\]\n\nFirst choose \\(\\delta\\), then \\(s\\). The same inequality with \\(A^*\\) controls the adjoint; exchanging the labels controls the lower edge. Interior norm-holomorphy gives the remaining continuity. The family consists of contractions, so differences have norm at most two. SF-2 upgrades this concrete joint strong-star continuity to joint intrinsic \\(\\sigma\\text{-strong-*}\\) continuity for every normal positive functional. This argument avoids both an unproved vector integration theorem and a weak-to-strong inference at a nonunitary endpoint.\n\n![Lower strip and exact Poisson kernels at two depths, with their full-line masses.](../assets/operator-reconstruction/assets/strip-kernels.png)\n\n*The strip is normalized to depth one. The plots are samples of the exact kernels (PS7); labels give the proved full-line masses (PS8), not the area of the displayed finite window. The endpoint limits follow from (PS9)–(PS11). Reproduction source: `render_figures.py`.*\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l158.form-necessity\"><\/a>\n\n<a id=\"OA-FLOW.XII5.STRIP.NECESSITY\"><\/a>\n<a id=\"oa-flow.xii5.strip.necessity\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>4. A proof of both boundary limits<\/h2>\n<p>Weak convergence at the lower edge would not suffice. We prove norm convergence on every vector and on every adjoint vector by explicit scalar kernels. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\)<\/span> define<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nP_s^0(r)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}{2(\\cosh(\\pi r)-\\cos(\\pi s))},\\qquad\nP_s^1(r)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}{2(\\cosh(\\pi r)+\\cos(\\pi s))}=P_{1-s}^0(r).\n\\tag{PS7}\n\\]\">\\[\nP_s^0(r)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}{2(\\cosh(\\pi r)-\\cos(\\pi s))},\\qquad\nP_s^1(r)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}{2(\\cosh(\\pi r)+\\cos(\\pi s))}=P_{1-s}^0(r).\n\\tag{PS7}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>They are positive. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta=\\pi s,\\quad v=e^{\\pi r}\\)\">\\(\\theta=\\pi s,\\quad v=e^{\\pi r}\\)<\/span>, direct one-variable integration gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}P_s^0(r)\\,dr\n=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty\n\\frac{dv}{(v-\\cos\\theta)^2+\\sin^2\\theta}\n=1-s,\n\\qquad\\int_{\\mathbb R}P_s^1(r)\\,dr=s.\n\\tag{PS8}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}P_s^0(r)\\,dr\n=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty\n\\frac{dv}{(v-\\cos\\theta)^2+\\sin^2\\theta}\n=1-s,\n\\qquad\\int_{\\mathbb R}P_s^1(r)\\,dr=s.\n\\tag{PS8}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)<\/span> the tail of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_s^0\\)\">\\(P_s^0\\)<\/span> outside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((-\\delta,\\delta)\\)\">\\((-\\delta,\\delta)\\)<\/span> is at most<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}2\\int_{|r|\\geq\\delta}\n\\frac{dr}{\\cosh(\\pi r)-1},\n\\tag{PS9}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(\\pi s)}2\\int_{|r|\\geq\\delta}\n\\frac{dr}{\\cosh(\\pi r)-1},\n\\tag{PS9}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>which tends to zero as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> decreases to zero. The fixed comparison integral is finite, away from its singular point and with exponential decay. At the lower edge exchange <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-s\\)\">\\(1-s\\)<\/span> and the two labels.<\/p>\n<p>For bounded continuous scalar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_0,b_1\\)\">\\(b_0,b_1\\)<\/span>, convolution with these two kernels is a bounded harmonic function of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((t,s)\\)\">\\((t,s)\\)<\/span>. To verify harmonicity directly, the kernels are respectively <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-1/2\\)\">\\(-1/2\\)<\/span> times the imaginary part of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\coth(\\pi(r+is)/2)\\)\">\\(\\coth(\\pi(r+is)/2)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/2\\)\">\\(1/2\\)<\/span> times the imaginary part of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tanh(\\pi(r+is)/2)\\)\">\\(\\tanh(\\pi(r+is)/2)\\)<\/span>. They have no poles in the open strip. On compact interior substrips, their derivatives through order two have integrable bounds with exponential tails. Differentiating the kernels in the convolution variable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t-x\\)\">\\(t-x\\)<\/span> under the scalar integral is therefore legitimate. The masses and (PS9) prove joint convergence to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_0(t_0)\\)\">\\(b_0(t_0)\\)<\/span> as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((t,s)\\to(t_0,0)\\)\">\\((t,s)\\to(t_0,0)\\)<\/span>: split the first convolution into small <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|r|\\)\">\\(|r|\\)<\/span>, controlled by continuity, and its tail, controlled by (PS9); the second convolution has mass <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. The same argument works at the bottom.<\/p>\n<p>The bounded harmonic function with these edge data is unique. For a bounded harmonic difference <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> with zero edge values, compare its real part and its negative with<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\epsilon\\cosh(ct)\\cos(c(s-1/2)),\\qquad 0&lt;c&lt;\\pi.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\epsilon\\cosh(ct)\\cos(c(s-1/2)),\\qquad 0&lt;c&lt;\\pi.\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This is positive harmonic on the closed strip. On the distant vertical sides of a sufficiently wide rectangle it exceeds <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\operatorname{Re}q|\\)\">\\(|\\operatorname{Re}q|\\)<\/span>; on the horizontal edges it exceeds zero. The rectangle harmonic maximum principle gives the comparison in the rectangle. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)<\/span> decrease to zero at each fixed point and repeat for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Im}q\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Im}q\\)<\/span>. Thus every bounded scalar holomorphic function continuous on the closed strip equals the stated convolution of its edges.<\/p>\n<p>Apply this scalar identity to the coefficients of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>. SF-3 constructs the required improper vector integrals directly, since the boundary orbits are bounded continuous and the kernels are continuous integrable. Equality of all scalar inner products proves<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(t-is)\\xi\n&amp;=\\int P_s^0(r)A(t-r)\\xi\\,dr+\\int P_s^1(r)B(t-r)\\xi\\,dr,\\\\\nV(t-is)^*\\xi\n&amp;=\\int P_s^0(r)A(t-r)^*\\xi\\,dr+\\int P_s^1(r)B(t-r)^*\\xi\\,dr.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{PS10}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(t-is)\\xi\n&amp;=\\int P_s^0(r)A(t-r)\\xi\\,dr+\\int P_s^1(r)B(t-r)\\xi\\,dr,\\\\\nV(t-is)^*\\xi\n&amp;=\\int P_s^0(r)A(t-r)^*\\xi\\,dr+\\int P_s^1(r)B(t-r)^*\\xi\\,dr.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{PS10}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For the second line pair the first with two vectors and use the real-valued kernels; both adjoint orbits are norm-continuous on each vector. The approximate-identity proof uses only the integral norm bound, so it now proves both vector limits jointly. More explicitly, for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|t-t_0|&lt;\\delta\\)\">\\(|t-t_0|&lt;\\delta\\)<\/span> and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\rho_\\xi(\\delta)=\\sup_{|q-t_0|\\leq2\\delta}\n\\|(A(q)-A(t_0))\\xi\\|,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\rho_\\xi(\\delta)=\\sup_{|q-t_0|\\leq2\\delta}\n\\|(A(q)-A(t_0))\\xi\\|,\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>one has<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|(V(t-is)-A(t_0))\\xi\\|\n\\leq\\rho_\\xi(\\delta)\n+2\\|\\xi\\|\\int_{|r|\\geq\\delta}P_s^0(r)\\,dr\n+2s\\|\\xi\\|.\n\\tag{PS11}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|(V(t-is)-A(t_0))\\xi\\|\n\\leq\\rho_\\xi(\\delta)\n+2\\|\\xi\\|\\int_{|r|\\geq\\delta}P_s^0(r)\\,dr\n+2s\\|\\xi\\|.\n\\tag{PS11}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>First choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\)\">\\(\\delta\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. The same inequality with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^*\\)\">\\(A^*\\)<\/span> controls the adjoint; exchanging the labels controls the lower edge. Interior norm-holomorphy gives the remaining continuity. The family consists of contractions, so differences have norm at most two. SF-2 upgrades this concrete joint strong-star continuity to joint intrinsic <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\text{-strong-*}\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\text{-strong-*}\\)<\/span> continuity for every normal positive functional. This argument avoids both an unproved vector integration theorem and a weak-to-strong inference at a nonunitary endpoint.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/operator-reconstruction/assets/strip-kernels.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/operator-reconstruction/assets/strip-kernels.png\" alt=\"Lower strip and exact Poisson kernels at two depths, with their full-line masses.\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><em>The strip is normalized to depth one. The plots are samples of the exact kernels (PS7); labels give the proved full-line masses (PS8), not the area of the displayed finite window. The endpoint limits follow from (PS9)–(PS11). Reproduction source: <code>render_figures.py<\/code>.<\/em><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L158.md","start_offset":7732,"end_offset":12620,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"9451c54aa7959410d6965042c629d9356476f9bbeea144fb47c5f861037486e8":{"markdown":"<a id=\"open-corner-generators\"><\/a>\n## The corner has only subgroup generators\n\nThe algebraic span of the monomials \\(\\pi(a)u_g\\), \\(a\\in M,g\\in G\\), is a unital star algebra: covariance verifies closure under multiplication and adjoints. It generates \\(P\\). If \\(g\\notin H\\), then \\(go\\ne o\\), and orthogonality gives\n\\[\n p\\pi(a)u_gp=0.\n \\tag{OS13}\n\\]\nFor \\(h\\in H\\), both \\(u_h\\) and \\(\\pi(a)\\) commute with \\(p\\). It follows that the corner is generated by\n\\[\n q(a)=p\\pi(\\widetilde a)p,\\qquad\n w_h=u_hp,\\qquad\n a\\in N,\\ h\\in H,\n \\tag{OS14}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\widetilde a\\in M\\) is \\(a\\) in coordinate \\(o\\) and zero elsewhere. For precision, bounded strong-star approximation of any \\(x\\in P\\) by elements of the algebraic star algebra, followed by compression, puts \\(pxp\\) in the strongly closed algebra generated by (OS14). The opposite inclusion is immediate. Thus this is an equality of von Neumann algebras, not just an algebraic spanning assertion.\n\nThe relations \\(w_hw_k=w_{hk}\\) and\n\\(w_hq(a)w_h^*=q(\\beta_h(a))\\) follow from covariance and (OS7). To identify this covariant representation as the faithful *normal regular* subgroup crossed product, we now give its whole Hilbert-space model.","html":"<h2>The corner has only subgroup generators<\/h2>\n<p>The algebraic span of the monomials <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(a)u_g\\)\">\\(\\pi(a)u_g\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M,g\\in G\\)\">\\(a\\in M,g\\in G\\)<\/span>, is a unital star algebra: covariance verifies closure under multiplication and adjoints. It generates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\notin H\\)\">\\(g\\notin H\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(go\\ne o\\)\">\\(go\\ne o\\)<\/span>, and orthogonality gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p\\pi(a)u_gp=0.\n \\tag{OS13}\n\\]\">\\[\n p\\pi(a)u_gp=0.\n \\tag{OS13}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in H\\)\">\\(h\\in H\\)<\/span>, both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_h\\)\">\\(u_h\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(a)\\)\">\\(\\pi(a)\\)<\/span> commute with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. It follows that the corner is generated by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n q(a)=p\\pi(\\widetilde a)p,\\qquad\n w_h=u_hp,\\qquad\n a\\in N,\\ h\\in H,\n \\tag{OS14}\n\\]\">\\[\n q(a)=p\\pi(\\widetilde a)p,\\qquad\n w_h=u_hp,\\qquad\n a\\in N,\\ h\\in H,\n \\tag{OS14}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde a\\in M\\)\">\\(\\widetilde a\\in M\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> in coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(o\\)\">\\(o\\)<\/span> and zero elsewhere. For precision, bounded strong-star approximation of any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in P\\)\">\\(x\\in P\\)<\/span> by elements of the algebraic star algebra, followed by compression, puts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(pxp\\)\">\\(pxp\\)<\/span> in the strongly closed algebra generated by (OS14). The opposite inclusion is immediate. Thus this is an equality of von Neumann algebras, not just an algebraic spanning assertion.<\/p>\n<p>The relations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_hw_k=w_{hk}\\)\">\\(w_hw_k=w_{hk}\\)<\/span> and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_hq(a)w_h^*=q(\\beta_h(a))\\)\">\\(w_hq(a)w_h^*=q(\\beta_h(a))\\)<\/span> follow from covariance and (OS7). To identify this covariant representation as the faithful <em>normal regular<\/em> subgroup crossed product, we now give its whole Hilbert-space model.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L55.md","start_offset":6576,"end_offset":7773,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"94f979e90ca1fe46758bcfab2dce73459a6365ad4e4dd0fa3e631f71ba7dc372":{"markdown":"<a id=\"pdm-4\"><\/a>\n## 4. The canonical phase determines the modulus\n\nThe scalar-normalized cocycle theorem [BC5](OA-FLOW-BC.md#oa-flow.bc.5), applied to (PDM3), gives\n\\[\n c_t=[D(\\psi\\circ\\alpha^{-1}):D\\psi]_t\n       =\\mu^{it}1.\n \\tag{PDM18}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\alpha\\) commutes with \\(\\sigma^\\psi\\), by modular covariance. The onto normal core automorphism proved in [CIM1](OA-FLOW-CIM.md#cim-1), [CIM2](OA-FLOW-CIM.md#cim-2) has the exact values\n\\[\n \\widetilde\\alpha(\\pi(x))=\\pi(\\alpha(x)),\\qquad\n \\widetilde\\alpha(u(t))=\\mu^{it}u(t).\n \\tag{PDM19}\n\\]\nIn the full regular Hilbert space its implementing unitary is simply\n\\[\n [V_\\psi(\\alpha)\\xi](r)=\\mu^{ir}U(\\alpha)\\xi(r),\n \\tag{PDM20}\n\\]\nwhere \\(U(\\alpha)\\) is the standard implementing unitary. This is an onto unitary, with inverse multiplier \\(\\mu^{-ir}U(\\alpha)^*\\). On translations its two scalar factors give\n\\(\\mu^{ir}\\mu^{-i(r-t)}=\\mu^{it}\\); on coefficients modular commutation gives \\(\\pi(\\alpha(x))\\). Its images generate the full core since the scalar factors in (PDM19) are invertible. Thus the phase has not been inferred merely from equality of modular groups.\n\nEvaluate on the full center generator:\n\\[\n \\widetilde\\alpha(z)=\\mu^{iP}z\n       =e^{-iP(-\\log\\mu)}z=\\theta_{-\\log\\mu}(z).\n \\tag{PDM21}\n\\]\nBoth maps are normal. By (PDM16), bounded Borel calculus, or ultraweak generation, their restrictions agree on the entire center. This proves (PDM4). [CIM3's exact chart square](OA-FLOW-CIM.md#cim-3) and [CORE8's isomorphism functor](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-8) transport the identity to the intrinsic core, independently of the faithful chart. Section 2 returns it to the original supported weight and automorphism.","html":"<h2>4. The canonical phase determines the modulus<\/h2>\n<p>The scalar-normalized cocycle theorem <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#oa-flow.bc.5\">BC5<\/a>, applied to (PDM3), gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c_t=[D(\\psi\\circ\\alpha^{-1}):D\\psi]_t\n       =\\mu^{it}1.\n \\tag{PDM18}\n\\]\">\\[\n c_t=[D(\\psi\\circ\\alpha^{-1}):D\\psi]_t\n       =\\mu^{it}1.\n \\tag{PDM18}\n\\]<\/span>\nConsequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma^\\psi\\)\">\\(\\sigma^\\psi\\)<\/span>, by modular covariance. The onto normal core automorphism proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CIM.html#cim-1\">CIM1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CIM.html#cim-2\">CIM2<\/a> has the exact values\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widetilde\\alpha(\\pi(x))=\\pi(\\alpha(x)),\\qquad\n \\widetilde\\alpha(u(t))=\\mu^{it}u(t).\n \\tag{PDM19}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widetilde\\alpha(\\pi(x))=\\pi(\\alpha(x)),\\qquad\n \\widetilde\\alpha(u(t))=\\mu^{it}u(t).\n \\tag{PDM19}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn the full regular Hilbert space its implementing unitary is simply\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n [V_\\psi(\\alpha)\\xi](r)=\\mu^{ir}U(\\alpha)\\xi(r),\n \\tag{PDM20}\n\\]\">\\[\n [V_\\psi(\\alpha)\\xi](r)=\\mu^{ir}U(\\alpha)\\xi(r),\n \\tag{PDM20}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U(\\alpha)\\)\">\\(U(\\alpha)\\)<\/span> is the standard implementing unitary. This is an onto unitary, with inverse multiplier <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^{-ir}U(\\alpha)^*\\)\">\\(\\mu^{-ir}U(\\alpha)^*\\)<\/span>. On translations its two scalar factors give\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^{ir}\\mu^{-i(r-t)}=\\mu^{it}\\)\">\\(\\mu^{ir}\\mu^{-i(r-t)}=\\mu^{it}\\)<\/span>; on coefficients modular commutation gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(\\alpha(x))\\)\">\\(\\pi(\\alpha(x))\\)<\/span>. Its images generate the full core since the scalar factors in (PDM19) are invertible. Thus the phase has not been inferred merely from equality of modular groups.<\/p>\n<p>Evaluate on the full center generator:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widetilde\\alpha(z)=\\mu^{iP}z\n       =e^{-iP(-\\log\\mu)}z=\\theta_{-\\log\\mu}(z).\n \\tag{PDM21}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widetilde\\alpha(z)=\\mu^{iP}z\n       =e^{-iP(-\\log\\mu)}z=\\theta_{-\\log\\mu}(z).\n \\tag{PDM21}\n\\]<\/span>\nBoth maps are normal. By (PDM16), bounded Borel calculus, or ultraweak generation, their restrictions agree on the entire center. This proves (PDM4). <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CIM.html#cim-3\">CIM3's exact chart square<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-8\">CORE8's isomorphism functor<\/a> transport the identity to the intrinsic core, independently of the faithful chart. Section 2 returns it to the original supported weight and automorphism.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-PDM.md","start_offset":11811,"end_offset":13488,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"949f2eec2c7de1c04828bb2d5087606d14baabc45e3d1d6ab5babe7849bf326a":{"markdown":"<a id=\"lac-local\"><\/a>\n## 3. Narrow spectral bands give central partial normalizers\n\n### Narrow polar decomposition\nWrite \\(M_\\phi(I)=\\{x:\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\subset I\\}\\). This subspace is weak-star closed when \\(I\\) is closed, by [GL2](OA-FLOW-GL.md#gl-2). In the limiting arguments below we use the actual closed spectrum of the element, contained in the indicated open window. Suppose\n\n\\[\n x\\in M_\\phi((t-\\mu_0/3,t+\\mu_0/3)).\n \\tag{LAC8}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(x=u|x|\\). Product spectral calculus puts \\(x^*x\\) in \\(M_\\phi((-2\\mu_0/3,2\\mu_0/3))=N\\). Hence \\(|x|\\in N\\), and\n\n\\[\n u=\\mathop{\\rm s\\!\\!\\!-lim}_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n       x(\\varepsilon+|x|)^{-1},\n \\qquad\n \\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(u)=\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x).\n \\tag{LAC9}\n\\]\n\nThe approximants in (LAC9) have spectrum inside the closed set \\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\); their bounded strong limit remains in that closed spectral subspace. The reverse spectral inclusion uses \\(x=u|x|\\) with a fixed-point right factor. The projections \\(e=u^*u=s_r(x)\\) and \\(e'=uu^*=s_\\ell(x)\\) lie in \\(N\\). Since the difference of two frequencies in (LAC8) lies inside the gap, \\(uNu^*\\subset N\\) and \\(u^*Nu\\subset N\\) on the corresponding corners.\n\nThe [narrow-band orbit theorem](OA-FLOW-L87.md#oa-flow.band.uniform) gives a complementary estimate. For a compact time set \\(K\\), a frequency \\(p\\), and \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), a sufficiently small neighborhood of \\(p\\) gives\n\\(\\|\\sigma_t^\\phi(x)-e^{-itp}x\\|\\le\\varepsilon\\|x\\|\\) for every \\(t\\in K\\) and every element in that band. Apply its positive-frequency convention at \\(-p\\). That earlier theorem is proved for arbitrary LCA groups; its real specialization here controls the whole compact time set without an eigenvector assumption.\n\n### Amplifying to central supports\nLet \\(c=z_N(e)\\) and \\(c'=z_N(e')\\). The exact countable comparison theorem used here says that in a countably decomposable properly infinite von Neumann algebra, a projection with central support \\(c\\) has countably many equivalent copies whose orthogonal sum is \\(c\\). Here is the required countable comparison even when \\(e\\) is finite. Work in \\(Nc\\) and set\n\\[\n B=Nc\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2\\mathbb N),\\qquad\n p=c\\otimes E_{11},\\quad q=e\\otimes1.\n \\tag{LACN2}\n\\]\nThe projection \\(p\\) is properly infinite because \\(Nc\\) is, by [PC5](OA-FLOW-PC.md#pc-5). The two disjoint shifts on the countable tensor factor make \\(q\\) properly infinite. Both have full central support by [CA0](OA-FLOW-CA.md#ca-0). Tensor a faithful normal state of \\(Nc\\) with a strictly positive summable diagonal state: vanishing of all positive diagonal compressions proves faithfulness. Thus \\(B\\) is countably decomposable. [PC7](OA-FLOW-PC.md#pc-7) and [PC3](OA-FLOW-PC.md#pc-3) give \\(p\\sim q\\). Transport the mutually orthogonal \\(e\\otimes E_{jj}\\) through this equivalence. They fill \\(p\\) and are each equivalent to \\(e\\otimes E_{11}\\), giving the required family in \\(pBp=Nc\\). Apply the same argument to \\(e'\\).\n\nChoose fixed-point partial isometries \\(a_j,b_j\\in N\\) with\n\n\\[\n a_j^*a_j=e,\\quad \\sum_j a_ja_j^*=c,\n \\qquad\n b_j^*b_j=e',\\quad \\sum_j b_jb_j^*=c',\n \\tag{LAC10}\n\\]\n\nwith orthogonal final projections in each family. Then the strong orthogonal sum\n\n\\[\n v=\\sum_j b_jua_j^*,\n \\qquad\n k=v^*x\n \\tag{LAC11}\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(v^*v=c\\) and \\(vv^*=c'\\). Fixed-point multiplication and weak-star closedness give \\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)\\subset\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\). Hence the spectrum of \\(k=v^*x\\) lies in the difference of the narrow window with itself, inside \\((-2\\mu_0/3,2\\mu_0/3)\\), so \\(k\\in N\\). Also \\(vk=vv^*x=c'x=x\\), because \\(c'\\) dominates the left support of \\(x\\). The same gap calculation gives both \\(vNv^*\\subset N\\) and \\(v^*Nv\\subset N\\). This is the centrally supported narrow-normalizer factorization; it does not require \\(v\\) to extend the original polar partial isometry.\n\n### Spectral support carriers and the corrected center map\nFor a spectral set \\(I\\), define\n\n\\[\n p_\\phi(I)=\\bigvee\\{s_\\ell(x):x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\},\n \\qquad\n q_\\phi(I)=\\bigvee\\{s_r(x):x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\}.\n \\tag{LAC12}\n\\]\n\nLeft and right multiplication by \\(N\\) preserves the spectral subspace. The [two support-join arguments FS1–3](OA-FLOW-FS.md#fs-1) therefore place both joins in \\(Z(N)\\).\n\nThe source asserts too much if it assumes only equal central initial supports. Indeed, take a narrow centrally supported normalizer \\(v\\), write \\(p=vv^*\\), and choose a proper isometry \\(a\\in Np\\) with \\(a^*a=p\\) and \\(aa^*<p\\). Then \\(w=av\\) has \\(w^*w=v^*v\\) and the same narrow spectrum, but \\(ww^*=aa^*\\neq p\\).\n\nThe correct statement requires both final supports to be central and one\ncommon narrow window. Suppose \\(v,w\\in M_\\phi(I)\\), with\n\\(I=(t-\\mu_0/3,t+\\mu_0/3)\\), have the same central initial support \\(q\\)\nand central final supports. More generally, it suffices that\n\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)-\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(w)\\subset(-\\mu_0,\\mu_0)\\).\nThe reflected difference gives the other mixed product. Then \\(vw^*\\in N\\) implements equivalence between those final central projections, so they are equal. Moreover \\(w^*v\\) is a unitary in \\(Nq\\), and for \\(z\\in Z(N)q\\),\n\n\\[\n vzv^*=wz w^*.\n \\tag{LAC13}\n\\]\n\nThus one common narrow spectral window canonically transports the appropriate initial central corner to its final central corner. Separate narrowness of the two spectra does not imply that their mixed products have frequency zero.\n\nThe statement also gives a map on the **whole support corners**, not only on one chosen partial normalizer. Put \\(q=q_\\phi(I)\\) and \\(p=p_\\phi(I)\\). If \\(M_\\phi(I)=\\{0\\}\\), both corners are zero and there is a unique map between them. Otherwise let \\(\\mathcal V_I\\) be the centrally supported partial normalizers with spectrum in \\(I\\). The factorization (LAC11), applied to every \\(x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\), gives\n\\[\n q=\\bigvee_{v\\in\\mathcal V_I}v^*v,\\qquad\n p=\\bigvee_{v\\in\\mathcal V_I}vv^*.\n \\tag{LACN7}\n\\]\nIndeed the factor of each \\(x\\) has supports dominating those of \\(x\\), while every member of \\(\\mathcal V_I\\) itself belongs to the spectral subspace.\n\nBy [DS2](OA-FLOW-DS.md#ds-2), \\(N\\) has a faithful normal state \\(\\omega\\). For each positive integer \\(n\\), normality supplies finitely many members of \\(\\mathcal V_I\\) whose initial-support join leaves \\(\\omega\\)-mass less than \\(2^{-n}\\) in \\(q\\), and finitely many whose final-support join leaves less than \\(2^{-n}\\) in \\(p\\). Enumerate their union as \\((v_j)\\). Faithfulness shows that their initial supports \\(r_j=v_j^*v_j\\) join to \\(q\\), and their final supports \\(p_j=v_jv_j^*\\) join to \\(p\\). A finite union is treated as a finite family.\n\nRefine the initial supports in that order:\n\\[\n d_j=r_j\\Bigl(1-\\bigvee_{i<j}r_i\\Bigr),\\qquad\n w_j=v_jd_j,\\qquad f_j=w_jw_j^*.\n \\tag{LACN8}\n\\]\nThe \\(d_j\\) are orthogonal central projections with sum \\(q\\). Both normalizing inclusions for \\(v_j\\) make conjugation an isomorphism \\(Nr_j\\to Np_j\\), with inverse conjugation by \\(v_j^*\\); hence \\(f_j\\) is central in \\(N\\). For \\(j\\ne k\\), the common window puts \\(w_j^*w_k\\) in \\(N\\), while its two central supports give\n\\(w_j^*w_k=d_j(w_j^*w_k)d_k=0\\). Thus the \\(f_j\\) are orthogonal.\n\nThey fill \\(p\\). For each \\(i,j\\), the two restrictions \\(v_i d_j\\) and \\(w_j r_i\\) have the same central initial support \\(r_i d_j\\), central final supports, and the same narrow window. Applying the already proved equal-support case of (LAC13) gives\n\\(v_i d_jv_i^*=w_jr_iw_j^*\\le f_j\\).\nSumming over \\(j\\) gives \\(p_i\\le\\sum_jf_j\\). Since the \\(p_i\\) join to \\(p\\), and each \\(f_j\\le p\\), their sum is exactly \\(p\\).\n\nThe full center map and its inverse are therefore\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\beta_I:Z(N)q&\\longrightarrow Z(N)p,\n &\\beta_I(z)&=\\sum_jw_jzw_j^*,\\\\\n \\beta_I^{-1}:Z(N)p&\\longrightarrow Z(N)q,\n &\\beta_I^{-1}(y)&=\\sum_jw_j^*yw_j.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{LACN9}\n\\]\nThese are bounded strong-star sums on orthogonal central corners, as in [PC1](OA-FLOW-PC.md#pc-1). On the \\(j\\)-th corner the two formulas are inverse normal conjugations. Products and adjoints are computed on each such corner; the orthogonal supports eliminate mixed products, and the two filling identities give both inverse identities on the whole centers. Each formula preserves bounded increasing positive suprema, because its compression to every filling corner does. Thus both maps are normal.\n\nThe choice of the countable family does not affect the map. For any \\(v\\in\\mathcal V_I\\), compare \\(vd_j\\) with \\(w_jv^*v\\) on their common initial support. Equation (LAC13) gives \\(\\beta_I(z)vd_j=vzd_j\\). Summing the orthogonal initial pieces yields \\(\\beta_I(z)v=vz\\). This identity determines \\(\\beta_I(z)\\) on every final support \\(vv^*\\), whose join is \\(p\\), so it determines the map uniquely. Its direction is from the right-support center to the left-support center; (LACN9) also supplies the reverse direction.\n\n\n### Positive, narrow, and primitive normalizers\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) consist of partial isometries \\(v\\in M_\\phi([\\mu_0,\\infty))\\) whose initial and final projections are central in \\(N\\) and for which both conjugation inclusions land in \\(N\\). Put\n\n\\[\n v_1\\preccurlyeq v_2\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n v_1^*v_1\\leq v_2^*v_2\n \\text{ and }v_1=v_2v_1^*v_1.\n \\tag{LAC14}\n\\]\n\nThis is a partial order. Orthogonal initial and final supports allow normalizers to be added, and the sum remains in \\(\\mathcal F\\). Positive modular spectrum and the preceding factorization show that \\(\\mathcal F\\) has nonzero members.\n\nDefine\n\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\mathcal H&=\\{0\\neq v\\in\\mathcal F:\n   \\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)-\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)\n       \\subset(-\\mu_0/2,\\mu_0/2)\\},\\\\\n \\mathcal G&=\\{v\\in\\mathcal H:\n   h_1h_2\\preccurlyeq v, h_i\\in\\mathcal H\n   \\Longrightarrow h_1h_2=0\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{LAC15}\n\\]\n\nElements of \\(\\mathcal G\\) are called primitive. This refers to ordered products of positive narrow normalizers, not to irreducibility in \\(M\\).","html":"<h2>3. Narrow spectral bands give central partial normalizers<\/h2>\n<h3>Narrow polar decomposition<\/h3>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_\\phi(I)=\\{x:\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\subset I\\}\\)\">\\(M_\\phi(I)=\\{x:\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\subset I\\}\\)<\/span>. This subspace is weak-star closed when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is closed, by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GL.html#gl-2\">GL2<\/a>. In the limiting arguments below we use the actual closed spectrum of the element, contained in the indicated open window. Suppose<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n x\\in M_\\phi((t-\\mu_0/3,t+\\mu_0/3)).\n \\tag{LAC8}\n\\]\">\\[\n x\\in M_\\phi((t-\\mu_0/3,t+\\mu_0/3)).\n \\tag{LAC8}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=u|x|\\)\">\\(x=u|x|\\)<\/span>. Product spectral calculus puts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^*x\\)\">\\(x^*x\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_\\phi((-2\\mu_0/3,2\\mu_0/3))=N\\)\">\\(M_\\phi((-2\\mu_0/3,2\\mu_0/3))=N\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|x|\\in N\\)\">\\(|x|\\in N\\)<\/span>, and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n u=\\mathop{\\rm s\\!\\!\\!-lim}_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n       x(\\varepsilon+|x|)^{-1},\n \\qquad\n \\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(u)=\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x).\n \\tag{LAC9}\n\\]\">\\[\n u=\\mathop{\\rm s\\!\\!\\!-lim}_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n       x(\\varepsilon+|x|)^{-1},\n \\qquad\n \\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(u)=\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x).\n \\tag{LAC9}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The approximants in (LAC9) have spectrum inside the closed set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\)<\/span>; their bounded strong limit remains in that closed spectral subspace. The reverse spectral inclusion uses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=u|x|\\)\">\\(x=u|x|\\)<\/span> with a fixed-point right factor. The projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e=u^*u=s_r(x)\\)\">\\(e=u^*u=s_r(x)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e&#x27;=uu^*=s_\\ell(x)\\)\">\\(e&#x27;=uu^*=s_\\ell(x)\\)<\/span> lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Since the difference of two frequencies in (LAC8) lies inside the gap, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(uNu^*\\subset N\\)\">\\(uNu^*\\subset N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*Nu\\subset N\\)\">\\(u^*Nu\\subset N\\)<\/span> on the corresponding corners.<\/p>\n<p>The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L87.html#oa-flow.band.uniform\">narrow-band orbit theorem<\/a> gives a complementary estimate. For a compact time set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, a frequency <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span>, a sufficiently small neighborhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\sigma_t^\\phi(x)-e^{-itp}x\\|\\le\\varepsilon\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\sigma_t^\\phi(x)-e^{-itp}x\\|\\le\\varepsilon\\|x\\|\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in K\\)\">\\(t\\in K\\)<\/span> and every element in that band. Apply its positive-frequency convention at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-p\\)\">\\(-p\\)<\/span>. That earlier theorem is proved for arbitrary LCA groups; its real specialization here controls the whole compact time set without an eigenvector assumption.<\/p>\n<h3>Amplifying to central supports<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=z_N(e)\\)\">\\(c=z_N(e)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c&#x27;=z_N(e&#x27;)\\)\">\\(c&#x27;=z_N(e&#x27;)\\)<\/span>. The exact countable comparison theorem used here says that in a countably decomposable properly infinite von Neumann algebra, a projection with central support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span> has countably many equivalent copies whose orthogonal sum is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span>. Here is the required countable comparison even when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> is finite. Work in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nc\\)\">\\(Nc\\)<\/span> and set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n B=Nc\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2\\mathbb N),\\qquad\n p=c\\otimes E_{11},\\quad q=e\\otimes1.\n \\tag{LACN2}\n\\]\">\\[\n B=Nc\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2\\mathbb N),\\qquad\n p=c\\otimes E_{11},\\quad q=e\\otimes1.\n \\tag{LACN2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> is properly infinite because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nc\\)\">\\(Nc\\)<\/span> is, by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#pc-5\">PC5<\/a>. The two disjoint shifts on the countable tensor factor make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> properly infinite. Both have full central support by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CA.html#ca-0\">CA0<\/a>. Tensor a faithful normal state of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nc\\)\">\\(Nc\\)<\/span> with a strictly positive summable diagonal state: vanishing of all positive diagonal compressions proves faithfulness. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> is countably decomposable. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#pc-7\">PC7<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#pc-3\">PC3<\/a> give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\sim q\\)\">\\(p\\sim q\\)<\/span>. Transport the mutually orthogonal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\otimes E_{jj}\\)\">\\(e\\otimes E_{jj}\\)<\/span> through this equivalence. They fill <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> and are each equivalent to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\otimes E_{11}\\)\">\\(e\\otimes E_{11}\\)<\/span>, giving the required family in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(pBp=Nc\\)\">\\(pBp=Nc\\)<\/span>. Apply the same argument to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e&#x27;\\)\">\\(e&#x27;\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose fixed-point partial isometries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_j,b_j\\in N\\)\">\\(a_j,b_j\\in N\\)<\/span> with<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n a_j^*a_j=e,\\quad \\sum_j a_ja_j^*=c,\n \\qquad\n b_j^*b_j=e&#x27;,\\quad \\sum_j b_jb_j^*=c&#x27;,\n \\tag{LAC10}\n\\]\">\\[\n a_j^*a_j=e,\\quad \\sum_j a_ja_j^*=c,\n \\qquad\n b_j^*b_j=e&#x27;,\\quad \\sum_j b_jb_j^*=c&#x27;,\n \\tag{LAC10}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>with orthogonal final projections in each family. Then the strong orthogonal sum<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n v=\\sum_j b_jua_j^*,\n \\qquad\n k=v^*x\n \\tag{LAC11}\n\\]\">\\[\n v=\\sum_j b_jua_j^*,\n \\qquad\n k=v^*x\n \\tag{LAC11}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v^*v=c\\)\">\\(v^*v=c\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*=c&#x27;\\)\">\\(vv^*=c&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Fixed-point multiplication and weak-star closedness give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)\\subset\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)\\subset\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(x)\\)<\/span>. Hence the spectrum of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=v^*x\\)\">\\(k=v^*x\\)<\/span> lies in the difference of the narrow window with itself, inside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((-2\\mu_0/3,2\\mu_0/3)\\)\">\\((-2\\mu_0/3,2\\mu_0/3)\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\in N\\)\">\\(k\\in N\\)<\/span>. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vk=vv^*x=c&#x27;x=x\\)\">\\(vk=vv^*x=c&#x27;x=x\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c&#x27;\\)\">\\(c&#x27;\\)<\/span> dominates the left support of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>. The same gap calculation gives both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vNv^*\\subset N\\)\">\\(vNv^*\\subset N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v^*Nv\\subset N\\)\">\\(v^*Nv\\subset N\\)<\/span>. This is the centrally supported narrow-normalizer factorization; it does not require <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> to extend the original polar partial isometry.<\/p>\n<h3>Spectral support carriers and the corrected center map<\/h3>\n<p>For a spectral set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>, define<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p_\\phi(I)=\\bigvee\\{s_\\ell(x):x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\},\n \\qquad\n q_\\phi(I)=\\bigvee\\{s_r(x):x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\}.\n \\tag{LAC12}\n\\]\">\\[\n p_\\phi(I)=\\bigvee\\{s_\\ell(x):x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\},\n \\qquad\n q_\\phi(I)=\\bigvee\\{s_r(x):x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\}.\n \\tag{LAC12}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Left and right multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> preserves the spectral subspace. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FS.html#fs-1\">two support-join arguments FS1–3<\/a> therefore place both joins in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(N)\\)\">\\(Z(N)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The source asserts too much if it assumes only equal central initial supports. Indeed, take a narrow centrally supported normalizer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span>, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p=vv^*\\)\">\\(p=vv^*\\)<\/span>, and choose a proper isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in Np\\)\">\\(a\\in Np\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^*a=p\\)\">\\(a^*a=p\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(aa^*&lt;p\\)\">\\(aa^*&lt;p\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w=av\\)\">\\(w=av\\)<\/span> has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w^*w=v^*v\\)\">\\(w^*w=v^*v\\)<\/span> and the same narrow spectrum, but <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ww^*=aa^*\\neq p\\)\">\\(ww^*=aa^*\\neq p\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The correct statement requires both final supports to be central and one\ncommon narrow window. Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v,w\\in M_\\phi(I)\\)\">\\(v,w\\in M_\\phi(I)\\)<\/span>, with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I=(t-\\mu_0/3,t+\\mu_0/3)\\)\">\\(I=(t-\\mu_0/3,t+\\mu_0/3)\\)<\/span>, have the same central initial support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>\nand central final supports. More generally, it suffices that\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)-\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(w)\\subset(-\\mu_0,\\mu_0)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)-\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(w)\\subset(-\\mu_0,\\mu_0)\\)<\/span>.\nThe reflected difference gives the other mixed product. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vw^*\\in N\\)\">\\(vw^*\\in N\\)<\/span> implements equivalence between those final central projections, so they are equal. Moreover <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w^*v\\)\">\\(w^*v\\)<\/span> is a unitary in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nq\\)\">\\(Nq\\)<\/span>, and for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in Z(N)q\\)\">\\(z\\in Z(N)q\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n vzv^*=wz w^*.\n \\tag{LAC13}\n\\]\">\\[\n vzv^*=wz w^*.\n \\tag{LAC13}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Thus one common narrow spectral window canonically transports the appropriate initial central corner to its final central corner. Separate narrowness of the two spectra does not imply that their mixed products have frequency zero.<\/p>\n<p>The statement also gives a map on the <strong>whole support corners<\/strong>, not only on one chosen partial normalizer. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=q_\\phi(I)\\)\">\\(q=q_\\phi(I)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p=p_\\phi(I)\\)\">\\(p=p_\\phi(I)\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_\\phi(I)=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(M_\\phi(I)=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, both corners are zero and there is a unique map between them. Otherwise let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal V_I\\)\">\\(\\mathcal V_I\\)<\/span> be the centrally supported partial normalizers with spectrum in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>. The factorization (LAC11), applied to every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\)\">\\(x\\in M_\\phi(I)\\)<\/span>, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n q=\\bigvee_{v\\in\\mathcal V_I}v^*v,\\qquad\n p=\\bigvee_{v\\in\\mathcal V_I}vv^*.\n \\tag{LACN7}\n\\]\">\\[\n q=\\bigvee_{v\\in\\mathcal V_I}v^*v,\\qquad\n p=\\bigvee_{v\\in\\mathcal V_I}vv^*.\n \\tag{LACN7}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed the factor of each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> has supports dominating those of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, while every member of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal V_I\\)\">\\(\\mathcal V_I\\)<\/span> itself belongs to the spectral subspace.<\/p>\n<p>By <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DS.html#ds-2\">DS2<\/a>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> has a faithful normal state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>. For each positive integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, normality supplies finitely many members of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal V_I\\)\">\\(\\mathcal V_I\\)<\/span> whose initial-support join leaves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>-mass less than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-n}\\)\">\\(2^{-n}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>, and finitely many whose final-support join leaves less than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-n}\\)\">\\(2^{-n}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. Enumerate their union as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((v_j)\\)\">\\((v_j)\\)<\/span>. Faithfulness shows that their initial supports <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_j=v_j^*v_j\\)\">\\(r_j=v_j^*v_j\\)<\/span> join to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>, and their final supports <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_j=v_jv_j^*\\)\">\\(p_j=v_jv_j^*\\)<\/span> join to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. A finite union is treated as a finite family.<\/p>\n<p>Refine the initial supports in that order:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d_j=r_j\\Bigl(1-\\bigvee_{i&lt;j}r_i\\Bigr),\\qquad\n w_j=v_jd_j,\\qquad f_j=w_jw_j^*.\n \\tag{LACN8}\n\\]\">\\[\n d_j=r_j\\Bigl(1-\\bigvee_{i&lt;j}r_i\\Bigr),\\qquad\n w_j=v_jd_j,\\qquad f_j=w_jw_j^*.\n \\tag{LACN8}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j\\)\">\\(d_j\\)<\/span> are orthogonal central projections with sum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>. Both normalizing inclusions for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_j\\)\">\\(v_j\\)<\/span> make conjugation an isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nr_j\\to Np_j\\)\">\\(Nr_j\\to Np_j\\)<\/span>, with inverse conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_j^*\\)\">\\(v_j^*\\)<\/span>; hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_j\\)\">\\(f_j\\)<\/span> is central in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\ne k\\)\">\\(j\\ne k\\)<\/span>, the common window puts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_j^*w_k\\)\">\\(w_j^*w_k\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, while its two central supports give\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_j^*w_k=d_j(w_j^*w_k)d_k=0\\)\">\\(w_j^*w_k=d_j(w_j^*w_k)d_k=0\\)<\/span>. Thus the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_j\\)\">\\(f_j\\)<\/span> are orthogonal.<\/p>\n<p>They fill <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i,j\\)\">\\(i,j\\)<\/span>, the two restrictions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_i d_j\\)\">\\(v_i d_j\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_j r_i\\)\">\\(w_j r_i\\)<\/span> have the same central initial support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_i d_j\\)\">\\(r_i d_j\\)<\/span>, central final supports, and the same narrow window. Applying the already proved equal-support case of (LAC13) gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_i d_jv_i^*=w_jr_iw_j^*\\le f_j\\)\">\\(v_i d_jv_i^*=w_jr_iw_j^*\\le f_j\\)<\/span>.\nSumming over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_i\\le\\sum_jf_j\\)\">\\(p_i\\le\\sum_jf_j\\)<\/span>. Since the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_i\\)\">\\(p_i\\)<\/span> join to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, and each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_j\\le p\\)\">\\(f_j\\le p\\)<\/span>, their sum is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The full center map and its inverse are therefore\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\beta_I:Z(N)q&amp;\\longrightarrow Z(N)p,\n &amp;\\beta_I(z)&amp;=\\sum_jw_jzw_j^*,\\\\\n \\beta_I^{-1}:Z(N)p&amp;\\longrightarrow Z(N)q,\n &amp;\\beta_I^{-1}(y)&amp;=\\sum_jw_j^*yw_j.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{LACN9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\beta_I:Z(N)q&amp;\\longrightarrow Z(N)p,\n &amp;\\beta_I(z)&amp;=\\sum_jw_jzw_j^*,\\\\\n \\beta_I^{-1}:Z(N)p&amp;\\longrightarrow Z(N)q,\n &amp;\\beta_I^{-1}(y)&amp;=\\sum_jw_j^*yw_j.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{LACN9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThese are bounded strong-star sums on orthogonal central corners, as in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#pc-1\">PC1<\/a>. On the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span>-th corner the two formulas are inverse normal conjugations. Products and adjoints are computed on each such corner; the orthogonal supports eliminate mixed products, and the two filling identities give both inverse identities on the whole centers. Each formula preserves bounded increasing positive suprema, because its compression to every filling corner does. Thus both maps are normal.<\/p>\n<p>The choice of the countable family does not affect the map. For any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in\\mathcal V_I\\)\">\\(v\\in\\mathcal V_I\\)<\/span>, compare <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vd_j\\)\">\\(vd_j\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_jv^*v\\)\">\\(w_jv^*v\\)<\/span> on their common initial support. Equation (LAC13) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_I(z)vd_j=vzd_j\\)\">\\(\\beta_I(z)vd_j=vzd_j\\)<\/span>. Summing the orthogonal initial pieces yields <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_I(z)v=vz\\)\">\\(\\beta_I(z)v=vz\\)<\/span>. This identity determines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_I(z)\\)\">\\(\\beta_I(z)\\)<\/span> on every final support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*\\)\">\\(vv^*\\)<\/span>, whose join is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, so it determines the map uniquely. Its direction is from the right-support center to the left-support center; (LACN9) also supplies the reverse direction.<\/p>\n<h3>Positive, narrow, and primitive normalizers<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal F\\)\">\\(\\mathcal F\\)<\/span> consist of partial isometries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in M_\\phi([\\mu_0,\\infty))\\)\">\\(v\\in M_\\phi([\\mu_0,\\infty))\\)<\/span> whose initial and final projections are central in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> and for which both conjugation inclusions land in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Put<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n v_1\\preccurlyeq v_2\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n v_1^*v_1\\leq v_2^*v_2\n \\text{ and }v_1=v_2v_1^*v_1.\n \\tag{LAC14}\n\\]\">\\[\n v_1\\preccurlyeq v_2\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n v_1^*v_1\\leq v_2^*v_2\n \\text{ and }v_1=v_2v_1^*v_1.\n \\tag{LAC14}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This is a partial order. Orthogonal initial and final supports allow normalizers to be added, and the sum remains in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal F\\)\">\\(\\mathcal F\\)<\/span>. Positive modular spectrum and the preceding factorization show that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal F\\)\">\\(\\mathcal F\\)<\/span> has nonzero members.<\/p>\n<p>Define<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\mathcal H&amp;=\\{0\\neq v\\in\\mathcal F:\n   \\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)-\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)\n       \\subset(-\\mu_0/2,\\mu_0/2)\\},\\\\\n \\mathcal G&amp;=\\{v\\in\\mathcal H:\n   h_1h_2\\preccurlyeq v, h_i\\in\\mathcal H\n   \\Longrightarrow h_1h_2=0\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{LAC15}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\mathcal H&amp;=\\{0\\neq v\\in\\mathcal F:\n   \\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)-\\operatorname{Sp}_\\phi(v)\n       \\subset(-\\mu_0/2,\\mu_0/2)\\},\\\\\n \\mathcal G&amp;=\\{v\\in\\mathcal H:\n   h_1h_2\\preccurlyeq v, h_i\\in\\mathcal H\n   \\Longrightarrow h_1h_2=0\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{LAC15}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal G\\)\">\\(\\mathcal G\\)<\/span> are called primitive. This refers to ordered products of positive narrow normalizers, not to irreducibility in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-LAC.md","start_offset":19055,"end_offset":28937,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"94ee17a240036f1d6ff3a8567a2e1318d285256aef23822bd6cb9191c676290d":{"markdown":"<a id=\"mgo-grades\"><\/a>\n## 4. Closed grades and their zero-real-part boundary\n\nSuppose additionally that \\(N^\\theta=M\\). Define\n\\[\n S_\\alpha=\\{T\\in S(N,\\tau):\\theta_s(T)=e^{-\\alpha s}T\n                    \\text{ for every }s\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n                                                                    \\tag{MG8}\n\\]\nEvery \\(S_\\alpha\\) is closed in measure: for each fixed \\(s\\), both sides of its defining equation are continuous functions of \\(T\\), by Section 3. Intersect their equalizers over all \\(s\\). No continuity in the time parameter on the whole algebra is used. Closed algebra operations give\n\\[\n S_\\alpha S_\\beta\\subset S_{\\alpha+\\beta},\\qquad\n S_\\alpha^*=S_{\\bar\\alpha}.\n                                                                    \\tag{MG9}\n\\]\nIf a nonzero \\(T\\) had two grades, their scalar exponential characters would agree for every real \\(s\\); differentiation at zero gives equality of the grades.\n\nWrite \\(T=v|T|\\), \\(\\alpha=p+it\\). Uniqueness of the polar decomposition, including its supports, gives\n\\[\n \\theta_s(|T|)=e^{-ps}|T|,\\qquad\n \\theta_s(v)=e^{-its}v.\n\\]\nFor \\(d_T(a)=\\tau(1_{(a,\\infty)}(|T|))\\), spectral transport gives\n\\[\n d_T(e^{ps}a)=e^{-s}d_T(a).                                      \\tag{MG10}\n\\]\nIf \\(p<0\\) and \\(T\\) is bounded and nonzero, preservation of its norm under \\(\\theta_s\\) contradicts its nonunit scalar scaling. If \\(T\\) is unbounded and measurable, choose \\(a\\) with \\(0<d_T(a)<\\infty\\), using (MG5). For \\(s>0\\), the left side of (MG10) is at least \\(d_T(a)\\), whereas its right side is strictly smaller. Thus \\(S_\\alpha=\\{0\\}\\) when \\(\\Re\\alpha<0\\).\n\nIf \\(p=0\\), choose a high spectral projection of \\(|T|\\) with finite trace. It is fixed by \\(\\theta_s\\), so its trace equals \\(e^{-s}\\) times itself. Its trace, and hence the projection, is zero. Therefore \\(T\\) is bounded. We obtain\n\\[\n S_0=M,\\qquad\n S_{it}=\\{x\\in N:\\theta_s(x)=e^{-its}x\\text{ for all }s\\},\n                                                                    \\tag{MG11}\n\\]\nwith the operator norm on the latter bounded fiber.\n\nFor a continuous core, let \\(h_\\psi\\) be the supported density of a faithful normal semifinite weight. Faithfulness makes \\(h_\\psi^{it}\\) a unitary, even if \\(\\psi(1)=\\infty\\). The core spectral relation gives\n\\(\\theta_s(h_\\psi^{it})=e^{-its}h_\\psi^{it}\\). Hence\n\\[\n M\\longrightarrow S_{it},\\quad x\\longmapsto xh_\\psi^{it}\n                                                                    \\tag{MG12}\n\\]\nis an onto linear isometry, with inverse \\(T\\mapsto Th_\\psi^{-it}\\). Indeed the product in the inverse is bounded and fixed. This argument uses no faithful normal state and no assertion that \\(h_\\psi\\) itself is measurable. The density spectral relation and full domains are those of [supported intrinsic-core coordinates](OA-FLOW-SCW.md#scw-2).","html":"<h2>4. Closed grades and their zero-real-part boundary<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose additionally that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N^\\theta=M\\)\">\\(N^\\theta=M\\)<\/span>. Define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n S_\\alpha=\\{T\\in S(N,\\tau):\\theta_s(T)=e^{-\\alpha s}T\n                    \\text{ for every }s\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n                                                                    \\tag{MG8}\n\\]\">\\[\n S_\\alpha=\\{T\\in S(N,\\tau):\\theta_s(T)=e^{-\\alpha s}T\n                    \\text{ for every }s\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n                                                                    \\tag{MG8}\n\\]<\/span>\nEvery <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_\\alpha\\)\">\\(S_\\alpha\\)<\/span> is closed in measure: for each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>, both sides of its defining equation are continuous functions of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>, by Section 3. Intersect their equalizers over all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. No continuity in the time parameter on the whole algebra is used. Closed algebra operations give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n S_\\alpha S_\\beta\\subset S_{\\alpha+\\beta},\\qquad\n S_\\alpha^*=S_{\\bar\\alpha}.\n                                                                    \\tag{MG9}\n\\]\">\\[\n S_\\alpha S_\\beta\\subset S_{\\alpha+\\beta},\\qquad\n S_\\alpha^*=S_{\\bar\\alpha}.\n                                                                    \\tag{MG9}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf a nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> had two grades, their scalar exponential characters would agree for every real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>; differentiation at zero gives equality of the grades.<\/p>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T=v|T|\\)\">\\(T=v|T|\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha=p+it\\)\">\\(\\alpha=p+it\\)<\/span>. Uniqueness of the polar decomposition, including its supports, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\theta_s(|T|)=e^{-ps}|T|,\\qquad\n \\theta_s(v)=e^{-its}v.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\theta_s(|T|)=e^{-ps}|T|,\\qquad\n \\theta_s(v)=e^{-its}v.\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_T(a)=\\tau(1_{(a,\\infty)}(|T|))\\)\">\\(d_T(a)=\\tau(1_{(a,\\infty)}(|T|))\\)<\/span>, spectral transport gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d_T(e^{ps}a)=e^{-s}d_T(a).                                      \\tag{MG10}\n\\]\">\\[\n d_T(e^{ps}a)=e^{-s}d_T(a).                                      \\tag{MG10}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&lt;0\\)\">\\(p&lt;0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> is bounded and nonzero, preservation of its norm under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s\\)\">\\(\\theta_s\\)<\/span> contradicts its nonunit scalar scaling. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> is unbounded and measurable, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;d_T(a)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(0&lt;d_T(a)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, using (MG5). For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s&gt;0\\)\">\\(s&gt;0\\)<\/span>, the left side of (MG10) is at least <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_T(a)\\)\">\\(d_T(a)\\)<\/span>, whereas its right side is strictly smaller. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_\\alpha=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(S_\\alpha=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Re\\alpha&lt;0\\)\">\\(\\Re\\alpha&lt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p=0\\)\">\\(p=0\\)<\/span>, choose a high spectral projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|T|\\)\">\\(|T|\\)<\/span> with finite trace. It is fixed by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s\\)\">\\(\\theta_s\\)<\/span>, so its trace equals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}\\)\">\\(e^{-s}\\)<\/span> times itself. Its trace, and hence the projection, is zero. Therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span> is bounded. We obtain\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n S_0=M,\\qquad\n S_{it}=\\{x\\in N:\\theta_s(x)=e^{-its}x\\text{ for all }s\\},\n                                                                    \\tag{MG11}\n\\]\">\\[\n S_0=M,\\qquad\n S_{it}=\\{x\\in N:\\theta_s(x)=e^{-its}x\\text{ for all }s\\},\n                                                                    \\tag{MG11}\n\\]<\/span>\nwith the operator norm on the latter bounded fiber.<\/p>\n<p>For a continuous core, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_\\psi\\)\">\\(h_\\psi\\)<\/span> be the supported density of a faithful normal semifinite weight. Faithfulness makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_\\psi^{it}\\)\">\\(h_\\psi^{it}\\)<\/span> a unitary, even if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi(1)=\\infty\\)\">\\(\\psi(1)=\\infty\\)<\/span>. The core spectral relation gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s(h_\\psi^{it})=e^{-its}h_\\psi^{it}\\)\">\\(\\theta_s(h_\\psi^{it})=e^{-its}h_\\psi^{it}\\)<\/span>. Hence\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n M\\longrightarrow S_{it},\\quad x\\longmapsto xh_\\psi^{it}\n                                                                    \\tag{MG12}\n\\]\">\\[\n M\\longrightarrow S_{it},\\quad x\\longmapsto xh_\\psi^{it}\n                                                                    \\tag{MG12}\n\\]<\/span>\nis an onto linear isometry, with inverse <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\mapsto Th_\\psi^{-it}\\)\">\\(T\\mapsto Th_\\psi^{-it}\\)<\/span>. Indeed the product in the inverse is bounded and fixed. This argument uses no faithful normal state and no assertion that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_\\psi\\)\">\\(h_\\psi\\)<\/span> itself is measurable. The density spectral relation and full domains are those of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SCW.html#scw-2\">supported intrinsic-core coordinates<\/a>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-MGO.md","start_offset":9840,"end_offset":12652,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"943c512f2d0aa003df770b8e815c929607636e9734d0450e5b6c9b4d5e99642e":{"markdown":"<a id=\"real-restriction-pair\"><\/a>\n## 8. The two restrictions and their compatibility\n\nRetain the Polish central-extension data of R9, with\n\\(L=\\Gamma\\times\\mathbb R\\) and trivial real action on \\(N\\).\nWrite \\(\\theta_t\\) for the action on \\(A\\). The \\(\\Gamma\\)-action\ncommutes with \\(\\theta\\). From a full factor pair put\n\\[\n h_g(n)=\\ell_{(g,0)}(n),\\qquad\n c_t(n)=\\ell_{(1,t)}(n),\\qquad\n \\chi=[m,h]\\in\\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A).\n \\tag{R10.1}\n\\]\nThe groups \\(\\mathscr H_\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\mathscr H_L\\) use the\nnormalized Borel factors and coboundaries of\n[BEX, Section 3](OA-FLOW-BEX.md#ext-gauge).\nFor \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\), act on a \\(\\Gamma\\)-pair by applying\n\\(\\theta_t\\) to both of its values. This preserves all factor\nidentities, since \\(\\theta_t\\) commutes with \\(\\Gamma\\).\nIt preserves coboundaries as well:\n\\(\\theta_t(\\partial b)=\\partial(\\theta_t b)\\).\nThus it defines an action on \\(\\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A)\\).\n\nLet \\(Z=Z^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\), \\(B=\\delta_A(A)\\), and\n\\(H=Z/B\\), with the topologies of R5–R6.\nThe action \\(g[c]=[t\\mapsto g(c_t)]\\) on \\(H\\) is well defined.\nHere \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Gamma(N,H)\\) denotes the\n\\(\\Gamma\\)-equivariant group homomorphisms; no standard Borel\nstructure on a possibly non-Hausdorff \\(H\\) is assumed.\n\n**Theorem R10 (restriction pair and exact lift criterion).**\nEvery full class determines, independently of its representative,\n\\[\n \\mathcal R:\\mathscr H_{\\Gamma\\times\\mathbb R}(N,A)\n \\longrightarrow\n \\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A)^{\\mathbb R}\n       \\times\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Gamma(N,H),\\qquad\n [m,\\ell]\\longmapsto(\\chi,\\nu),\\quad\n \\nu(n)=[c_\\bullet(n)].\n \\tag{R10.2}\n\\]\nThis is a homomorphism of abelian groups. Its produced \\(\\nu\\)\nis continuous for the quotient topology on \\(H\\) and has a\nBorel lift \\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\in Z\\).\n\nA pair \\((\\chi,\\nu)\\) is in its image if and only if there are\na normalized Borel \\(\\Gamma\\)-pair \\((m,h)\\) representing \\(\\chi\\)\nand a jointly Borel map \\(c:\\mathbb R\\times N\\to A\\) such that,\nfor every \\(n,p\\in N\\), \\(g\\in\\Gamma\\), and \\(s,t\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n c_0(n)&=1,&c_t(1)&=1,&\n c_{s+t}(n)&=c_s(n)\\theta_s(c_t(n)),\\\\\n [c_\\bullet(n)]&=\\nu(n),\\\\\n c_t(n)c_t(p)c_t(np)^{-1}\n      &=\\theta_t(m(n,p))m(n,p)^{-1},\\\\\n g(c_t(n))c_t(gn)^{-1}\n      &=\\theta_t(h_g(n))h_g(n)^{-1}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{R10.3}\n\\]\nEach path in this criterion is automatically continuous.\nThe requirement is one jointly Borel family satisfying these\nidentities at every displayed argument. Separate choices of a\ncoboundary for each time do not establish it.\n\n*Proof of the forward statements.* Restricting the full identities\n(EX3) to \\(\\Gamma\\) gives the pair \\((m,h)\\). Restricting their\naction identity to the real factor gives the first line of\n(R10.3). The [semidirect-product argument in BEX](OA-FLOW-BEX.md#ext-real)\nmakes each Borel cocycle path continuous. R6's rational-evaluation\nargument makes \\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\) Borel into the Polish\npath group \\(Z\\).\n\nThe mixed identity for the real action, whose action on \\(N\\)\nis trivial, gives the third line of (R10.3). Compute the factor\nof the same action element in its two commuting orders:\n\\[\n \\ell_{(g,t)}(n)\n   =g(c_t(n))h_g(n)\n   =\\theta_t(h_g(n))c_t(gn).\n \\tag{R10.4}\n\\]\nThis gives the last line of (R10.3). These two lines say exactly\n\\[\n  (\\theta_t m,\\theta_t h)=(m,h)^{\\,c_t},\n  \\qquad c_t:N\\to A,\\quad c_t(1)=1,\n \\tag{R10.5}\n\\]\nwhere the exponent denotes the gauge formulas (EX8), restricted\nto \\(\\Gamma\\). Each \\(c_t\\) is Borel. Hence \\(\\theta_t\\chi=\\chi\\)\nfor every \\(t\\).\n\nThe third line of (R10.3) says that the multiplicative defect\nof \\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\) is the coboundary\n\\(\\delta_A(m(n,p))\\). Thus \\(\\nu(np)=\\nu(n)\\nu(p)\\).\nThe last line says that \\(g(c_\\bullet(n))\\) and\n\\(c_\\bullet(gn)\\) differ by the coboundary\n\\(\\delta_A(h_g(n))\\), proving \\(\\Gamma\\)-equivariance.\n\nUnder a normalized Borel change \\(b:N\\to A\\), (EX8) gives\n\\[\n (m,h)\\longmapsto(m,h)^b,\\qquad\n c_t(n)\\longmapsto\n c_t^b(n)=b(n)^{-1}\\theta_t(b(n))c_t(n).\n \\tag{R10.6}\n\\]\nBoth \\(\\chi\\) and \\(\\nu\\) are unchanged. Pointwise multiplication\nof full pairs multiplies both restrictions, which proves that\n\\(\\mathcal R\\) is a homomorphism.\nFinally R9 realizes the full extension as Polish, and R6 gives\ncontinuous displacement. The composite\n\\(E\\to Z\\to H\\) is constant on the cosets of \\(A\\) and induces\nexactly \\(\\nu:E/A\\to H\\). The quotient map \\(E\\to N\\) is open,\nso \\(\\nu\\) is continuous, even when \\(H\\) is not Hausdorff.\n\n*Proof of sufficiency.* Start with the data in (R10.3).\nThe \\(\\Gamma\\)-pair constructs the Borel group \\(E_m\\) and its\n\\(\\Gamma\\)-action, by (EX5). On this same group define\n\\[\n S_g(a,n)=(g(a)h_g(n),gn),\\qquad\n \\Theta_t(a,n)=(\\theta_t(a)c_t(n),n).\n \\tag{R10.7}\n\\]\nThe real cocycle law makes \\(\\Theta_s\\Theta_t=\\Theta_{s+t}\\);\nnormalization makes \\(\\Theta_0\\) the identity. Its inverse is\ntherefore \\(\\Theta_{-t}\\).\nThe first coordinates of \\(\\Theta_t((a,n)(b,p))\\) and\n\\(\\Theta_t(a,n)\\Theta_t(b,p)\\) are respectively\n\\[\n \\theta_t(ab)\\theta_t(m(n,p))c_t(np),\\qquad\n \\theta_t(ab)c_t(n)c_t(p)m(n,p).\n\\]\nThey agree by (R10.3). Thus each \\(\\Theta_t\\) is an automorphism.\nThe first coordinates of \\(S_g\\Theta_t(a,n)\\) and\n\\(\\Theta_tS_g(a,n)\\), after cancelling the common factor\n\\(g(\\theta_t(a))=\\theta_t(g(a))\\), are\n\\(g(c_t(n))h_g(n)\\) and \\(\\theta_t(h_g(n))c_t(gn)\\).\nThey agree by the last line of (R10.3). Their second coordinates\nare both \\(gn\\). Consequently \\(S\\) and \\(\\Theta\\) commute.\n\nDefine the action of \\((g,t)\\) to be \\(S_g\\Theta_t\\).\nIt is jointly Borel, since all its displayed factors are jointly\nBorel. It is an action of the direct product by automorphisms.\nIts full factor is\n\\[\n \\ell_{(g,t)}(n)=g(c_t(n))h_g(n).\n \\tag{R10.8}\n\\]\nReading the product and action laws of this constructed group\nthrough its section \\(s(n)=(1,n)\\) proves all of (EX3)–(EX4);\nthe reconstruction calculations in BEX apply verbatim.\nR9 now supplies the unique compatible Polish topology and\njoint continuity, with the original topologies on \\(A,N\\).\nThe restrictions are the given \\((m,h)\\) and \\(c\\), so the\nresulting class maps to \\((\\chi,\\nu)\\). This proves sufficiency\nand, with the forward construction, necessity. \\(\\square\\)\n\n![The exact compatibility between the two lifted actions](../assets/borel-extension-realization/restriction-compatibility.png)\n\n*Commuting lifts on the same reconstructed group.*\nEvery vertex is \\(E_m\\). The horizontal maps are \\(\\Theta_t\\);\nthe vertical maps are \\(S_g\\). Equality of the two paths is\nexactly (R10.4), after the common coefficient action is cancelled.\nThe displayed multiplication condition is the other compatibility\nequation in (R10.3). The proof is the calculation (R10.7)–(R10.8);\nthe diagram asserts no classification of von Neumann algebras.\n\nThe criterion does not depend on which representative of \\(\\chi\\)\nis chosen. Given a normalized Borel \\(b\\), use (R10.6); equivalently,\ntransport (R10.7) through the already proved gauge isomorphism\n\\(T_b:E_{m^b}\\to E_m\\). This preserves the action laws and gives\nexactly the two compatibility identities for the changed data.\nThe inverse change is \\(b^{-1}\\). It is the existence of the\ncompatible joint lift that is required, not merely\n\\(\\mathbb R\\)-invariance of the restricted class.\n\nFor a pair in the image, the full real-displacement requirement\nhas the particularly simple form\n\\[\n \\delta_E(E)=Z\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\nu(N)=H.\n \\tag{R10.9}\n\\]\nIndeed, (EX12) gives\n\\(\\delta_E(E)=\\bigcup_{n\\in N}B\\,c_\\bullet(n)\\).\nThis union is all of \\(Z\\) exactly when its cosets cover \\(Z/B\\).\nDensity of the image would not suffice. The shear of BEX,\nSection 6, has trivial \\(\\Gamma\\), \\(\\chi=1\\), and\n\\(\\nu(r)=[t\\mapsto e^{irt}]\\), which covers all of \\(H\\).\nRestricting its parameter group to discrete \\(\\mathbb Q\\)\nretains a valid restricted pair but loses (R10.9), as the\nirrational-frequency diagnostic there proves.","html":"<h2>8. The two restrictions and their compatibility<\/h2>\n<p>Retain the Polish central-extension data of R9, with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L=\\Gamma\\times\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(L=\\Gamma\\times\\mathbb R\\)<\/span> and trivial real action on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>.\nWrite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_t\\)\">\\(\\theta_t\\)<\/span> for the action on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. The <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-action\ncommutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span>. From a full factor pair put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n h_g(n)=\\ell_{(g,0)}(n),\\qquad\n c_t(n)=\\ell_{(1,t)}(n),\\qquad\n \\chi=[m,h]\\in\\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A).\n \\tag{R10.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n h_g(n)=\\ell_{(g,0)}(n),\\qquad\n c_t(n)=\\ell_{(1,t)}(n),\\qquad\n \\chi=[m,h]\\in\\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A).\n \\tag{R10.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe groups <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr H_\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\mathscr H_\\Gamma\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr H_L\\)\">\\(\\mathscr H_L\\)<\/span> use the\nnormalized Borel factors and coboundaries of\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BEX.html#ext-gauge\">BEX, Section 3<\/a>.\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, act on a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-pair by applying\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_t\\)\">\\(\\theta_t\\)<\/span> to both of its values. This preserves all factor\nidentities, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_t\\)\">\\(\\theta_t\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>.\nIt preserves coboundaries as well:\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_t(\\partial b)=\\partial(\\theta_t b)\\)\">\\(\\theta_t(\\partial b)=\\partial(\\theta_t b)\\)<\/span>.\nThus it defines an action on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A)\\)\">\\(\\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=Z^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\)\">\\(Z=Z^1_\\theta(\\mathbb R,A)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=\\delta_A(A)\\)\">\\(B=\\delta_A(A)\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=Z/B\\)\">\\(H=Z/B\\)<\/span>, with the topologies of R5–R6.\nThe action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g[c]=[t\\mapsto g(c_t)]\\)\">\\(g[c]=[t\\mapsto g(c_t)]\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is well defined.\nHere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Gamma(N,H)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Gamma(N,H)\\)<\/span> denotes the\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-equivariant group homomorphisms; no standard Borel\nstructure on a possibly non-Hausdorff <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is assumed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem R10 (restriction pair and exact lift criterion).<\/strong>\nEvery full class determines, independently of its representative,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mathcal R:\\mathscr H_{\\Gamma\\times\\mathbb R}(N,A)\n \\longrightarrow\n \\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A)^{\\mathbb R}\n       \\times\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Gamma(N,H),\\qquad\n [m,\\ell]\\longmapsto(\\chi,\\nu),\\quad\n \\nu(n)=[c_\\bullet(n)].\n \\tag{R10.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mathcal R:\\mathscr H_{\\Gamma\\times\\mathbb R}(N,A)\n \\longrightarrow\n \\mathscr H_\\Gamma(N,A)^{\\mathbb R}\n       \\times\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Gamma(N,H),\\qquad\n [m,\\ell]\\longmapsto(\\chi,\\nu),\\quad\n \\nu(n)=[c_\\bullet(n)].\n \\tag{R10.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a homomorphism of abelian groups. Its produced <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span>\nis continuous for the quotient topology on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> and has a\nBorel lift <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\in Z\\)\">\\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\in Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>A pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\chi,\\nu)\\)\">\\((\\chi,\\nu)\\)<\/span> is in its image if and only if there are\na normalized Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((m,h)\\)\">\\((m,h)\\)<\/span> representing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\)\">\\(\\chi\\)<\/span>\nand a jointly Borel map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c:\\mathbb R\\times N\\to A\\)\">\\(c:\\mathbb R\\times N\\to A\\)<\/span> such that,\nfor every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n,p\\in N\\)\">\\(n,p\\in N\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\in\\Gamma\\)\">\\(g\\in\\Gamma\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s,t\\in\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(s,t\\in\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n c_0(n)&amp;=1,&amp;c_t(1)&amp;=1,&amp;\n c_{s+t}(n)&amp;=c_s(n)\\theta_s(c_t(n)),\\\\\n [c_\\bullet(n)]&amp;=\\nu(n),\\\\\n c_t(n)c_t(p)c_t(np)^{-1}\n      &amp;=\\theta_t(m(n,p))m(n,p)^{-1},\\\\\n g(c_t(n))c_t(gn)^{-1}\n      &amp;=\\theta_t(h_g(n))h_g(n)^{-1}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{R10.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n c_0(n)&amp;=1,&amp;c_t(1)&amp;=1,&amp;\n c_{s+t}(n)&amp;=c_s(n)\\theta_s(c_t(n)),\\\\\n [c_\\bullet(n)]&amp;=\\nu(n),\\\\\n c_t(n)c_t(p)c_t(np)^{-1}\n      &amp;=\\theta_t(m(n,p))m(n,p)^{-1},\\\\\n g(c_t(n))c_t(gn)^{-1}\n      &amp;=\\theta_t(h_g(n))h_g(n)^{-1}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{R10.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nEach path in this criterion is automatically continuous.\nThe requirement is one jointly Borel family satisfying these\nidentities at every displayed argument. Separate choices of a\ncoboundary for each time do not establish it.<\/p>\n<p><em>Proof of the forward statements.<\/em> Restricting the full identities\n(EX3) to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> gives the pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((m,h)\\)\">\\((m,h)\\)<\/span>. Restricting their\naction identity to the real factor gives the first line of\n(R10.3). The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BEX.html#ext-real\">semidirect-product argument in BEX<\/a>\nmakes each Borel cocycle path continuous. R6's rational-evaluation\nargument makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\)\">\\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\)<\/span> Borel into the Polish\npath group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The mixed identity for the real action, whose action on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>\nis trivial, gives the third line of (R10.3). Compute the factor\nof the same action element in its two commuting orders:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\ell_{(g,t)}(n)\n   =g(c_t(n))h_g(n)\n   =\\theta_t(h_g(n))c_t(gn).\n \\tag{R10.4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\ell_{(g,t)}(n)\n   =g(c_t(n))h_g(n)\n   =\\theta_t(h_g(n))c_t(gn).\n \\tag{R10.4}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis gives the last line of (R10.3). These two lines say exactly\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n  (\\theta_t m,\\theta_t h)=(m,h)^{\\,c_t},\n  \\qquad c_t:N\\to A,\\quad c_t(1)=1,\n \\tag{R10.5}\n\\]\">\\[\n  (\\theta_t m,\\theta_t h)=(m,h)^{\\,c_t},\n  \\qquad c_t:N\\to A,\\quad c_t(1)=1,\n \\tag{R10.5}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere the exponent denotes the gauge formulas (EX8), restricted\nto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. Each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_t\\)\">\\(c_t\\)<\/span> is Borel. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_t\\chi=\\chi\\)\">\\(\\theta_t\\chi=\\chi\\)<\/span>\nfor every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The third line of (R10.3) says that the multiplicative defect\nof <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\)\">\\(n\\mapsto c_\\bullet(n)\\)<\/span> is the coboundary\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_A(m(n,p))\\)\">\\(\\delta_A(m(n,p))\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(np)=\\nu(n)\\nu(p)\\)\">\\(\\nu(np)=\\nu(n)\\nu(p)\\)<\/span>.\nThe last line says that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g(c_\\bullet(n))\\)\">\\(g(c_\\bullet(n))\\)<\/span> and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_\\bullet(gn)\\)\">\\(c_\\bullet(gn)\\)<\/span> differ by the coboundary\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_A(h_g(n))\\)\">\\(\\delta_A(h_g(n))\\)<\/span>, proving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-equivariance.<\/p>\n<p>Under a normalized Borel change <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b:N\\to A\\)\">\\(b:N\\to A\\)<\/span>, (EX8) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (m,h)\\longmapsto(m,h)^b,\\qquad\n c_t(n)\\longmapsto\n c_t^b(n)=b(n)^{-1}\\theta_t(b(n))c_t(n).\n \\tag{R10.6}\n\\]\">\\[\n (m,h)\\longmapsto(m,h)^b,\\qquad\n c_t(n)\\longmapsto\n c_t^b(n)=b(n)^{-1}\\theta_t(b(n))c_t(n).\n \\tag{R10.6}\n\\]<\/span>\nBoth <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\)\">\\(\\chi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> are unchanged. Pointwise multiplication\nof full pairs multiplies both restrictions, which proves that\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal R\\)\">\\(\\mathcal R\\)<\/span> is a homomorphism.\nFinally R9 realizes the full extension as Polish, and R6 gives\ncontinuous displacement. The composite\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\to Z\\to H\\)\">\\(E\\to Z\\to H\\)<\/span> is constant on the cosets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and induces\nexactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu:E/A\\to H\\)\">\\(\\nu:E/A\\to H\\)<\/span>. The quotient map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\to N\\)\">\\(E\\to N\\)<\/span> is open,\nso <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> is continuous, even when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is not Hausdorff.<\/p>\n<p><em>Proof of sufficiency.<\/em> Start with the data in (R10.3).\nThe <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-pair constructs the Borel group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_m\\)\">\\(E_m\\)<\/span> and its\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-action, by (EX5). On this same group define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n S_g(a,n)=(g(a)h_g(n),gn),\\qquad\n \\Theta_t(a,n)=(\\theta_t(a)c_t(n),n).\n \\tag{R10.7}\n\\]\">\\[\n S_g(a,n)=(g(a)h_g(n),gn),\\qquad\n \\Theta_t(a,n)=(\\theta_t(a)c_t(n),n).\n \\tag{R10.7}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe real cocycle law makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_s\\Theta_t=\\Theta_{s+t}\\)\">\\(\\Theta_s\\Theta_t=\\Theta_{s+t}\\)<\/span>;\nnormalization makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_0\\)\">\\(\\Theta_0\\)<\/span> the identity. Its inverse is\ntherefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_{-t}\\)\">\\(\\Theta_{-t}\\)<\/span>.\nThe first coordinates of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_t((a,n)(b,p))\\)\">\\(\\Theta_t((a,n)(b,p))\\)<\/span> and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_t(a,n)\\Theta_t(b,p)\\)\">\\(\\Theta_t(a,n)\\Theta_t(b,p)\\)<\/span> are respectively\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\theta_t(ab)\\theta_t(m(n,p))c_t(np),\\qquad\n \\theta_t(ab)c_t(n)c_t(p)m(n,p).\n\\]\">\\[\n \\theta_t(ab)\\theta_t(m(n,p))c_t(np),\\qquad\n \\theta_t(ab)c_t(n)c_t(p)m(n,p).\n\\]<\/span>\nThey agree by (R10.3). Thus each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_t\\)\">\\(\\Theta_t\\)<\/span> is an automorphism.\nThe first coordinates of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_g\\Theta_t(a,n)\\)\">\\(S_g\\Theta_t(a,n)\\)<\/span> and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_tS_g(a,n)\\)\">\\(\\Theta_tS_g(a,n)\\)<\/span>, after cancelling the common factor\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g(\\theta_t(a))=\\theta_t(g(a))\\)\">\\(g(\\theta_t(a))=\\theta_t(g(a))\\)<\/span>, are\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g(c_t(n))h_g(n)\\)\">\\(g(c_t(n))h_g(n)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_t(h_g(n))c_t(gn)\\)\">\\(\\theta_t(h_g(n))c_t(gn)\\)<\/span>.\nThey agree by the last line of (R10.3). Their second coordinates\nare both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gn\\)\">\\(gn\\)<\/span>. Consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta\\)\">\\(\\Theta\\)<\/span> commute.<\/p>\n<p>Define the action of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((g,t)\\)\">\\((g,t)\\)<\/span> to be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_g\\Theta_t\\)\">\\(S_g\\Theta_t\\)<\/span>.\nIt is jointly Borel, since all its displayed factors are jointly\nBorel. It is an action of the direct product by automorphisms.\nIts full factor is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\ell_{(g,t)}(n)=g(c_t(n))h_g(n).\n \\tag{R10.8}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\ell_{(g,t)}(n)=g(c_t(n))h_g(n).\n \\tag{R10.8}\n\\]<\/span>\nReading the product and action laws of this constructed group\nthrough its section <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(n)=(1,n)\\)\">\\(s(n)=(1,n)\\)<\/span> proves all of (EX3)–(EX4);\nthe reconstruction calculations in BEX apply verbatim.\nR9 now supplies the unique compatible Polish topology and\njoint continuity, with the original topologies on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A,N\\)\">\\(A,N\\)<\/span>.\nThe restrictions are the given <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((m,h)\\)\">\\((m,h)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span>, so the\nresulting class maps to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\chi,\\nu)\\)\">\\((\\chi,\\nu)\\)<\/span>. This proves sufficiency\nand, with the forward construction, necessity. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/borel-extension-realization/restriction-compatibility.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/borel-extension-realization/restriction-compatibility.png\" alt=\"The exact compatibility between the two lifted actions\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><em>Commuting lifts on the same reconstructed group.<\/em>\nEvery vertex is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_m\\)\">\\(E_m\\)<\/span>. The horizontal maps are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_t\\)\">\\(\\Theta_t\\)<\/span>;\nthe vertical maps are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_g\\)\">\\(S_g\\)<\/span>. Equality of the two paths is\nexactly (R10.4), after the common coefficient action is cancelled.\nThe displayed multiplication condition is the other compatibility\nequation in (R10.3). The proof is the calculation (R10.7)–(R10.8);\nthe diagram asserts no classification of von Neumann algebras.<\/p>\n<p>The criterion does not depend on which representative of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\)\">\\(\\chi\\)<\/span>\nis chosen. Given a normalized Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, use (R10.6); equivalently,\ntransport (R10.7) through the already proved gauge isomorphism\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_b:E_{m^b}\\to E_m\\)\">\\(T_b:E_{m^b}\\to E_m\\)<\/span>. This preserves the action laws and gives\nexactly the two compatibility identities for the changed data.\nThe inverse change is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b^{-1}\\)\">\\(b^{-1}\\)<\/span>. It is the existence of the\ncompatible joint lift that is required, not merely\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>-invariance of the restricted class.<\/p>\n<p>For a pair in the image, the full real-displacement requirement\nhas the particularly simple form\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\delta_E(E)=Z\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\nu(N)=H.\n \\tag{R10.9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\delta_E(E)=Z\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\nu(N)=H.\n \\tag{R10.9}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed, (EX12) gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_E(E)=\\bigcup_{n\\in N}B\\,c_\\bullet(n)\\)\">\\(\\delta_E(E)=\\bigcup_{n\\in N}B\\,c_\\bullet(n)\\)<\/span>.\nThis union is all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> exactly when its cosets cover <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z/B\\)\">\\(Z/B\\)<\/span>.\nDensity of the image would not suffice. The shear of BEX,\nSection 6, has trivial <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi=1\\)\">\\(\\chi=1\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(r)=[t\\mapsto e^{irt}]\\)\">\\(\\nu(r)=[t\\mapsto e^{irt}]\\)<\/span>, which covers all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>.\nRestricting its parameter group to discrete <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb Q\\)\">\\(\\mathbb Q\\)<\/span>\nretains a valid restricted pair but loses (R10.9), as the\nirrational-frequency diagnostic there proves.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-BRL.md","start_offset":49080,"end_offset":56938,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
